// A borrow BOUND TO A NAME is still the lender's object, not a copy of it.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM write_through_whole.cell. That file
// measures what happens when a borrow is passed DIRECTLY as a call argument,
// `bump(&mut buf)`. This one measures the other half of the same rule: a
// borrow parked in a local first,
//
//     let exclusive e = &mut buf
//     bump(exclusive e)
//
// which every call through `e` must forward to `buf` and not to a copy of it.
// The two are different code paths in both new backends, and the argument path
// being right says nothing about the binding path.
//
// examples/nll_dead_borrow.cell has carried this exact shape since 41abc3b and
// could never have caught the defect, because its `grow` has an empty body and
// writes nothing. A test can carry a defect's exact shape and still be blind to
// it: what makes a shape testable is that something OBSERVABLE depends on it.
//
// WHAT WENT WRONG, measured at 9e591ab. `llvmemit.zig`'s slot-shape predicate
// read `i < f.param_count and ...`. It made a claim about PARAMETERS and
// applied it to every binding, so a let-bound borrow got a struct-shaped slot,
// was initialized with a LOADED COPY of the lender, and handed that copy's
// address to every later call. The write landed in the copy:
//
//     C backend     38 for the single-bump form, since 8b82b62
//     MLIR backend  38, since e7605ac, which fixed the same predicate there
//     LLVM backend  37, silently, with no diagnostic from anything
//
// A SECOND DEFECT LIVES IN THE SAME PROGRAM, and it is why `doubled` and `snap`
// are here rather than a bare `print_int(buf.len)`. Once a borrow's slot holds
// an ADDRESS, every position that wants the VALUE has to load through it.
// `llvmemit.zig` loaded through in two positions and not the rest, so
// `let copy c = b` stored an address into a struct-shaped slot and
// `sum(copy b)` passed pointer bits as the argument. This program printed
// 25320060605 through that backend while the other two printed 125. That is a
// worse failure than a lost write and it is invisible to any check that only
// asks whether the write landed, which is why both shapes are pinned here.
//
// WHAT IT PRINTS, and how to read a wrong answer. One integer, `122`:
//
//   `buf.len` starts at 37, `buf.step` is 1, and the three let-bound spellings
//   below each add `step`, so `buf.len` is 40. `doubled` returns
//   `(len + step) * 2` = 82 by two different by-value routes, and `snap.len` is
//   40, for 122.
//
//   A spelling whose write is LOST costs 3 (2 from `doubled`, 1 from
//   `snap.len`), so 119, 116 and 113 each name a count of broken spellings,
//   and 113 means all three. A number in the billions means an address was
//   read where an aggregate belonged, which is the second defect rather than
//   the first.
//
// WHY THREE SPELLINGS AND NOT FIVE, and both reasons are defects in the
// REFERENCE backend rather than choices. examples/borrows.cell declares five
// unique-borrow spellings identical AS CALL ARGUMENTS, and
// write_through_whole.cell exercises all five there. In a `let` INITIALIZER
// `codegen.zig` breaks the group in two different ways, so two spellings are
// left out rather than pinned to an answer this file would then have to bless:
//
//   `let exclusive e = exclusive buf`   emits `cell_Buffer e = buf;`, a COPY,
//                                       where the sigil forms emit a pointer.
//                                       Borrowck disagrees and creates a real
//                                       exclusive loan for it: `let copy n =
//                                       buf.len` after one is refused with
//                                       "cannot use 'buf.len' while it is
//                                       exclusively borrowed". So C prints 37
//                                       for the single-bump form where LLVM
//                                       and MLIR print 38, and C is the one
//                                       that is wrong.
//   `let exclusive e = exclusive &buf`  emits `const cell_Buffer *e = &buf;`.
//                                       The keyword is supposed to win over
//                                       the sigil, which is what
//                                       examples/borrows.cell says of the
//                                       argument form, and here it does not.
//                                       Loud rather than silent: the gate's
//                                       AddressSanitizer stage compiles at
//                                       -Werror and reports
//                                       -Wincompatible-pointer-types-discards-qualifiers
//                                       at the call, which is how this was
//                                       found.
//
// Both are lowered correctly by the LLVM backend, and a unit test in
// src/cell/llvmemit.zig pins all FIVE spellings emitting byte-identical IR
// there, so the coverage this file gives up is kept where it can be kept.
//
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: 122

pub struct Buffer {
    copy len: Int,
    copy step: Int,
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

// The mutator writes the WHOLE value rather than a field, because the MLIR
// backend refuses a field-path assignment and this file is in the corpus those
// two backends must agree on. Reading `b.step` on the way in forces the callee
// to index the borrowed object at an offset past the first, where a wrong base
// and a right base cannot coincide.
pub fn bump(exclusive b: Buffer) {
    b = Buffer { len: b.len + b.step, step: b.step }
}

pub fn sum(copy b: Buffer) -> Int {
    return b.len + b.step
}

// A borrow consumed BY VALUE, twice, by the two routes that differ in the
// emitted IR: once through a `copy` binding of the borrow, and once by passing
// the borrow straight into a `copy` parameter. The C backend spells these
// `cell_Buffer c = *b;` and `cell_sum(*b)`, so both are a load THROUGH the
// borrow and neither is the borrow itself.
pub fn doubled(exclusive b: Buffer) -> Int {
    let copy c = b
    return sum(copy c) + sum(copy b)
}

pub fn main() {
    var owned buf = Buffer { len: 37, step: 1 }

    // Three sigil spellings of a unique borrow, each parked in a name before
    // the call. `&mut` is the Rust spelling, `&var` is the CELL v2.0 one, and
    // `&exclusive` is this language's keyword sigil. All three reach a backend
    // wrapped in a unary borrow node, and a backend that handles one and not
    // another splits the group without saying so. The two spellings this file
    // cannot carry are named in the header, with the reason.
    let exclusive e1 = &mut buf
    bump(exclusive e1)
    let exclusive e2 = &var buf
    bump(exclusive e2)
    let exclusive e3 = &exclusive buf
    bump(exclusive e3)

    // buf.len is 40 here. Both by-value routes inside `doubled` read it
    // through the borrow.
    let copy twice = doubled(exclusive buf)

    // And the same by-value question asked of a LET-BOUND borrow rather than a
    // parameter, which is a different slot and was a different bug.
    let exclusive v = &mut buf
    let copy snap = v

    print_int(twice + snap.len)
}
